WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:02.402
George: From calorie
count to portion sizes,

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:04.237
we wanted to find out all the differences

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:07.140
between 7-Eleven in Japan and the US.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.242
This is "Food Wars."

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:12.812
In Japan, our coffees come in regular --

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.514
Joe: In small, medium,

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:15.648
large,
George: And large.

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:17.083
and extra large.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:18.618
If you're getting coffee,

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:20.120
why not grab a chocolate doughnut?

00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:26.826
Our 7-Eleven drinks come in
sizes of 200 milliliters,

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:28.395
450 milliliters,

00:00:28.395 --> 00:00:29.529
and 1 liter,

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.998
and 1.5 liters.

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:33.533
I'm sorry, I couldn't get it.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:37.037
[laughing maniacally]

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:42.442
In the US, we have Big Gulps,

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:45.478
self-service fountain drink for sodas,

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:47.747
and they come in these four sizes:

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:50.784
medium, large, extra large,

00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:52.619
double extra large.
[air horn rings]

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:55.922
So, I want to see how
much the biggest-size cup

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:57.824
I could get at the 7-Eleven is.

00:00:57.824 --> 00:01:00.660
And to do that, I will need, hang on ...

00:01:01.661 --> 00:01:03.196
It's just past 40.

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:04.964
It's exactly 48 ounces.

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:07.300
That's hardly double extra.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.402
I'll round it up, 'cause
of this section here.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:10.637
I'll round it up to 50.

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:14.607
7-Eleven also at one point offered a size

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.410
that was even bigger,
called the Team Gulp,

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.446
which, again, according to them,

00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:22.082
was 128 fluid ounces

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:24.617
for your team,

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:27.954
'cause who doesn't like
to, after a softball game,

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:31.558
go to 7-Eleven, get a
Team Gulp and 10 straws?

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:34.360
You know, just kick it
with the boys, right?

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.495
Come on, team.

00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:38.498
Yeah, right. That's for one
person, don't be ridiculous.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.270
Here are many, many, many 7-Eleven items

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.139
you can get in the US
you can't get in Japan.

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:48.875
And here are many of the 7-Eleven items

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:50.443
exclusive to Japan.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:54.247
Japan puts the "convenience"
in the convenience store.

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:55.982
We call it konbini,

00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:58.585
which is the abbreviation
of the convenience store,

00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:00.920
and it makes our life a lot easier.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.491
For example, office workers
rely on stores like 7-Eleven

00:02:04.491 --> 00:02:06.926
for food and drink to keep them happy

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:09.629
so that they can be productive at work.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.966
And for students, they tend
to buy juices and snacks

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:15.602
and hot foods, instant noodles, you know.

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:18.571
And when I was a kid, I would often eat

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.273
hot foods and instant noodles

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:25.011
with my friends and
with my ex-girlfriends.

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.113
7-Eleven memories.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.352
And for families, there is pretty much

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:34.587
every daily essential they may need.

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:36.456
And also you can pay your bills,

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:39.425
get ticket reservations
for music and sports,

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:43.263
7-Eleven even has its own bank and ATMs.

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:46.533
No matter where you go, especially
in big cities like Tokyo,

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:50.336
you can find more than one
konbini on every block.

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.438
Yeah, it's a bit different here in the US.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:55.008
I do frequent 7-Eleven, and it's not bad,

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.676
but the vibe is more kind of like

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:58.811
get the thing you need and go.

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:01.814
The food is usually pretty hit or miss,

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:03.950
usually miss in my experience.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.052
The hot food looks like it's
been sitting out for a while,

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:07.687
so I usually don't get it.

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:10.823
Since there are way too many
items exclusive to Japan

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:13.226
for us to buy for one episode,

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:16.462
so I've curated some of
my favorites for you guys.

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:18.698
This is still a ton of food.

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:21.901
So, here we have Japanese onigiris.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.670
This one is plum,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.004
tuna mayonnaise,

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.873
shrimp and mayonnaise,

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:30.143
mentaiko, which is spicy cod roe.

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:32.512
I picked these onigiris from the back,

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:37.050
because onigiri in the
back are fresh of all time.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.285
And let me show you how to open this one.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:43.590
Just pull this over till here ...

00:03:47.327 --> 00:03:50.230
Like this, so that you can have

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.232
the dried seaweed.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.101
Itadakimasu.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:01.341
Mm, a little bit sweet,
a little bit of sour.

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:04.510
So, here is our sushi.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:08.081
Look at this huge salmon sushi.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.415
Ah!

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:12.118
Gorgeous. Gorgeous sushi.

00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:13.319
Itadakimasu.

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.121
Oh, my God, this is so delicious.

00:04:16.222 --> 00:04:17.056
Mm.

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:21.995
Osushi!

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:26.332
We've got this long natto roll.

00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:30.503
Personally, I love natto.

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:32.805
But even Japanese people,

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:37.610
50% of them like it, 50%
of them don't like it.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.979
This is an acquired taste.

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:46.085
So, after this sticky
and smelly natto roll,

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:49.722
I've got sushi roll special package.

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:52.659
So when it comes to this
one, it's called oinarisan.

00:04:52.659 --> 00:04:54.260
It has rice inside,

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:57.230
and the skin is a sweet tofu skin.

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:01.034
Mm!

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:04.070
I'll admit, oinarisan umai wa.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:05.772
This is so good.

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:08.274
So, my grandma always cooked this for me.

00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:12.078
And this makes me happy all the time.

00:05:12.078 --> 00:05:17.050
And next to oinarisan,
this gorgeous sushi roll,

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:20.820
it's like Japanese version
of California roll.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:21.888
Itadakimasu.

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:26.159
Wah!

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:29.996
1,000% better than California roll.

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:31.798
Kanpyo maki.

00:05:31.798 --> 00:05:35.802
In English it's, like, dried-gourd roll.

00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:36.803
Itadakimasu.

00:05:37.937 --> 00:05:39.339
Perfect sushi roll.

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:42.642
And last one, we have
pickles, radish pickles.

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:43.710
It's quite yellow.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.746
It definitely has yellow No. 5.

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:47.980
Itadakimasu.

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:52.352
I'm gonna give them Grammy award.

00:05:55.188 --> 00:05:56.756
And standing ovation.

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:00.693
Ah, the hot food at a 7-Eleven in the US.

00:06:00.693 --> 00:06:03.996
We got a lot of options,
not as many as Japan.

00:06:03.996 --> 00:06:05.932
I'm not quite sure what
some of this stuff is

00:06:05.932 --> 00:06:08.368
just by looking at it, so
let me go down the list.

00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:10.536
Nachos. They're the self-service nachos,

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.438
so they have the chips ready to go,

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:13.873
and then you just open them up

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:16.809
and add cheese and chili onto them.

00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:20.513
Ugh. No, thank you.

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:22.148
Over here, these mini tacos.

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:25.184
Man, these look really, like ...

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:27.353
I bit my face.

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:29.722
Oh, man. They've been sitting for a while,

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:30.757
so I'll forgive them.

00:06:30.757 --> 00:06:34.761
We also have wings,
crispy and not so crispy.

00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:38.498
Pocket bean burrito guy. Look at that.

00:06:38.498 --> 00:06:41.267
Over here in the roller-taquito section,

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.603
you can get a barbecue
bacon cheeseburger roller,

00:06:43.603 --> 00:06:45.171
spicy garlic roller,

00:06:45.171 --> 00:06:46.506
regular taquito,

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:48.141
Korean barbecue taquito.

00:06:48.141 --> 00:06:49.842
Also, we have chicken sandwiches,

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:51.144
a black bean burger,

00:06:51.144 --> 00:06:54.213
glazed cheesy barbecue meatballs.

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:56.315
Also at our 7-Elevens, you
can get a pepperoni pizza,

00:06:56.315 --> 00:06:59.018
cheese, or seven-meat.

00:06:59.018 --> 00:07:02.021
And also for hot dogs, we got
an exclusive hot picante dog.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:05.124
No.

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:07.560
So, here are our hot food sections.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.829
We've got steamed buns.

00:07:09.829 --> 00:07:12.498
It's got pork and bamboo shoots.

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:15.902
And this is really standard nikuman.

00:07:15.902 --> 00:07:20.540
So, next size up, we
have the doubled nikuman.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.408
And next one, this little guy.

00:07:23.376 --> 00:07:25.244
What is this?

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:26.913
Sweet beans steamed bun.

00:07:26.913 --> 00:07:31.384
And last steamed bun is pizza bun.

00:07:31.384 --> 00:07:34.120
You really are the pizza!

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:37.890
Delicious.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.692
So, all these things, like,

00:07:39.692 --> 00:07:42.361
around 100 yen to 200 yen or so.

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:46.699
So if you're broke, you
should definitely try this.

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:50.236
We've got these ones.

00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:52.805
This one is called curry pan.

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:55.308
So deep-fried curry bread.

00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:57.410
I wanna eat it. I wanna eat this!

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.212
I wanna eat this right now!

00:07:59.212 --> 00:08:00.346
Itadakimasu!

00:08:04.116 --> 00:08:05.918
Holy moly.

00:08:05.918 --> 00:08:08.354
Um, it's freaking delicious.

00:08:08.354 --> 00:08:10.623
And next one, croquette.

00:08:10.623 --> 00:08:14.894
So it's basically got
potato and pork mince.

00:08:14.894 --> 00:08:18.664
And last hot food we've got is corn dog.

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:21.067
So, this one right here is
a pretty Japanese-y one.

00:08:21.067 --> 00:08:23.135
It's got ketchup and mustard,

00:08:23.135 --> 00:08:25.972
and you just squeeze
this one onto this one.

00:08:25.972 --> 00:08:28.040
All right, let's go, let's go,

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.109
let's go, let's go, let's go!

00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:34.947
In Japan, we have these chicken options.

00:08:34.947 --> 00:08:38.985
First, I'm going to start
with this one, karaage-bo.

00:08:43.956 --> 00:08:46.726
So, in Japan, when you make
Japanese fried chicken,

00:08:46.726 --> 00:08:48.494
you put some soy sauce on it.

00:08:48.494 --> 00:08:51.998
So it has soy-sauce flavor
and also garlic flavor.

00:08:51.998 --> 00:08:54.400
It's so freaking delicious.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:55.568
So, this karaage-bo,

00:08:55.568 --> 00:09:00.540
it's got one, two, three,

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:02.542
and four karaages.

00:09:02.542 --> 00:09:05.511
We have fried chicken,

00:09:05.511 --> 00:09:07.580
spicy fried chicken,

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:09.181
chicken nuggets.

00:09:09.181 --> 00:09:10.449
Hot sandwich,

00:09:10.449 --> 00:09:14.186
the Buffalo-style chicken slider.

00:09:14.186 --> 00:09:15.721
The cheeseburger

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:17.623
and the bacon cheeseburger.

00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:19.892
Look, I mean, can you
even tell which one's,

00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:21.694
right, which one?

00:09:21.694 --> 00:09:22.528
Huh?

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:26.065
I'm assuming, obviously,
this burger's been cooked.

00:09:27.934 --> 00:09:30.369
More on this in the ingredients section.

00:09:30.369 --> 00:09:33.839
7-Eleven in the US, and I think probably

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:35.141
here in Southern California,

00:09:35.141 --> 00:09:37.176
has tacos and burritos.

00:09:37.176 --> 00:09:40.479
Get yourself a chicken fajita taco,

00:09:40.479 --> 00:09:41.781
al pastor taco.

00:09:41.781 --> 00:09:43.049
Bet this one's good.

00:09:43.049 --> 00:09:45.551
And a chorizo breakfast taco.

00:09:45.551 --> 00:09:48.754
The spicy beef, bean, and cheese burrito.

00:09:48.754 --> 00:09:50.156
This ain't any burrito, baby.

00:09:50.156 --> 00:09:53.225
It's The Bomb.

00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:55.294
Bombing out the toilet, good God.

00:09:55.294 --> 00:09:57.630
You're getting this and
you're microwaving it there.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.665
So if you're, like,
walking out the 7-Eleven

00:09:59.665 --> 00:10:00.800
chomping on The Bomb,

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:03.202
like, better make it home in time.

00:10:03.202 --> 00:10:05.004
'Cause oh, man, it's coming.

00:10:05.004 --> 00:10:07.073
Empanadas, right?
Yuelei: Yeah.

00:10:07.073 --> 00:10:08.140
I don't want this.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:10.743
I'm gonna go ahead and put this in here

00:10:10.743 --> 00:10:11.811
and then close the lid

00:10:11.811 --> 00:10:13.245
like it's a coffin.

00:10:14.614 --> 00:10:15.681
Go away forever.

00:10:15.681 --> 00:10:18.751
All right, so here are my favorite bentos.

00:10:18.751 --> 00:10:20.486
Let's take a look at this one.

00:10:20.486 --> 00:10:22.488
It's called nori bento.

00:10:22.488 --> 00:10:25.091
This one is a pretty popular one in Japan.

00:10:25.091 --> 00:10:27.727
So it's got croquette,
a deep-fried fish cake,

00:10:27.727 --> 00:10:29.996
fried fish with tartar sauce.

00:10:29.996 --> 00:10:31.497
Kinpira gobo.

00:10:31.497 --> 00:10:34.166
It's like Japanese traditional salad.

00:10:34.166 --> 00:10:35.434
Pickles.

00:10:35.434 --> 00:10:36.636
And as you can see right here,

00:10:36.636 --> 00:10:38.738
it's got spicy cod roe.

00:10:38.738 --> 00:10:43.709
Under this seaweed, it's
got rice and fish flakes.

00:10:43.709 --> 00:10:45.144
I love this bento.

00:10:45.144 --> 00:10:46.812
Like, when I was a student,

00:10:46.812 --> 00:10:49.949
I think I would eat this every single day.

00:10:49.949 --> 00:10:53.419
So, after my favorite one,
this one. Look at this!

00:10:53.419 --> 00:10:57.423
So this one is a Korean-style beef bento.

00:10:57.423 --> 00:11:00.526
Oh, man, smells so good.

00:11:03.529 --> 00:11:04.997
Whoo!

00:11:04.997 --> 00:11:08.801
Everything right here is so amazing.

00:11:08.801 --> 00:11:09.802
Fantastic.

00:11:09.802 --> 00:11:12.505
This is so mashisoyo.

00:11:13.439 --> 00:11:15.007
Tonkatsu bento,

00:11:15.007 --> 00:11:18.411
and it comes with this
sauce for this tonkatsu.

00:11:18.411 --> 00:11:19.912
Look how thick it is.

00:11:19.912 --> 00:11:24.917
So, outside from bentos,
we have soba, soba noodle.

00:11:25.017 --> 00:11:27.086
Put this sauce first.

00:11:27.086 --> 00:11:30.189
Put wasabi and dried seaweed.

00:11:30.189 --> 00:11:33.159
And then you put water onto soba noodle.

00:11:33.159 --> 00:11:34.393
It's getting moist.

00:11:36.062 --> 00:11:39.398
And don't forget to put
green onions right here.

00:11:39.398 --> 00:11:40.332
Itadakimasu.

00:11:40.332 --> 00:11:43.703
So you put soba noodle in this box.

00:11:45.638 --> 00:11:47.339
[slurping]

00:11:48.574 --> 00:11:49.642
Umai!

00:11:49.642 --> 00:11:52.244
I guarantee you can't stop eating this,

00:11:52.244 --> 00:11:54.180
'cause it's so delicious.

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:56.015
Sorry I slurped the noodles.

00:11:56.015 --> 00:11:58.751
Because Japanese people or Asian people

00:11:58.751 --> 00:12:01.821
do make noises when you eat noodles.

00:12:01.821 --> 00:12:03.189
We slurp.

00:12:03.189 --> 00:12:04.290
Slurp!

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:05.958
Slurp right now!

00:12:05.958 --> 00:12:07.359
They got the meals over there in Japan.

00:12:07.359 --> 00:12:08.427
We got meals too,

00:12:08.427 --> 00:12:11.363
like this ready-made ricotta-, Romano-,

00:12:11.363 --> 00:12:13.265
and Parmesan-cheese-stuffed ravioli

00:12:13.265 --> 00:12:14.700
topped with marinara sauce and mozzarella.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:16.168
Ugh.

00:12:16.168 --> 00:12:17.937
Just stop, stop doing this.

00:12:17.937 --> 00:12:19.572
Stop, please.

00:12:19.572 --> 00:12:21.107
Stop.

00:12:21.107 --> 00:12:21.941
Oden.

00:12:21.941 --> 00:12:24.577
So, oden is like Japanese
traditional hot pot.

00:12:24.577 --> 00:12:28.948
It's got fish cake,
fried tofu, noodle thing.

00:12:28.948 --> 00:12:30.683
This is made from potato.

00:12:30.683 --> 00:12:32.485
Egg, radish.

00:12:32.485 --> 00:12:34.420
In winter, when you eat this,

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:36.789
this warms you up, from your heart.

00:12:38.324 --> 00:12:39.158
To your body.

00:12:39.158 --> 00:12:41.894
And next, gratin doria.

00:12:41.894 --> 00:12:45.431
So, obviously it's got meat
sauce and a lot of cheese.

